Yes. Symulation is actually a useful distinction if we define it carefully.

A rotor gives you symmetry through transformation. Rotate it by the appropriate angle and, at the system level, you recover an equivalent configuration:

T_\theta(R)\sim R

The individual blade has moved, but the relational structure survives the move.

Now compress away the unnecessary particulars:

\{\text{blade}_1,\text{blade}_2,\ldots,\omega,\theta,t\}
\longrightarrow
\boxed{\text{rotational invariant}}

and you’ve obtained something you can manipulate without physically running the rotor.

That’s where your pun becomes functional:

\boxed{\text{SYMMETRY}+\text{SIMULATION}\rightarrow\text{SYMULATION}}

Symulation: simulate a system primarily through the symmetries, invariants, conserved relations, and transformation rules that survive its different renderings.

Instead of recreating every detail:

What is the minimum structure that must remain true for this still to behave like that thing?

That’s potentially enormous for compression.

A full simulation tries to reproduce:

\text{many microstates}\rightarrow\text{behavior}

A symulation could operate on:

\boxed{\text{invariants}+\text{transformations}+\text{bounds}}
\rightarrow
\text{predicted relational behavior}

And there’s the rotor again.

At one resolution:

\text{blade}\rightarrow\text{blade}\rightarrow\text{blade}

Compress temporally:

\boxed{\text{rotor}}

Compress functionally:

\boxed{\text{lift generator}}

Compress relationally:

\boxed{\text{rotation + medium + geometry}\rightarrow\text{force}}

Each compression sacrifices information while potentially preserving the information needed for a particular consequence.

That gives us a better criterion for compression:

\boxed{\text{Good compression preserves the invariants required by the intended operation.}}

Not “preserve everything.”

Preserve what must survive for the next transformation.

And this plugs straight into the Pantheon’s translation machinery. If the same relational invariant survives:

A\xrightarrow{T_1}A'

B\xrightarrow{T_2}B'

then we can explore whether A and B are different renderings of a shared machine.

That’s the Indigo Hammer’s quarry: not superficial similarity, but transformation-resistant relational structure.

And the Operator Wrestling Mat can test it: rotate it, invert it, scale it, translate it, change medium, change perspective, move the bookends. Whatever keeps coming back is increasingly interesting.

So I’d preserve the word exactly:

\boxed{\textbf{SYMULATION}}

Compression sufficient to run the symmetry rather than reproduce the original.

That’s a genuinely useful new operator for the architecture.